# Canonical multi-platform build runner

Implement `tools/build/adapter-inbound/run.py` to consume saved preflight and
architecture
decisions, revalidate them, build every selected target transactionally, and
publish only the minimal native deliverable under `dist/<ARCH>/` for each
successful architecture.

## Acceptance criteria

- [ ] Produce Android ARM64 as APK, iOS ARM64 as IPA, and native packaged
  outputs for selected Linux ARM64/AMD64, macOS ARM64, and Windows
  ARM64/AMD64 architectures.
- [x] Keep tvOS and macOS Intel / x86-64 outside the planned build matrix unless
  a future high-traction issue or pull request brings enough testing support to
  admit them explicitly.
- [x] Keep iOS packaging local and sideload-oriented with no App Store
  submission workflow; document unavoidable Apple SDK, signing, or
  build-host constraints instead of hiding them.
- [x] Use candidate output followed by atomic publication so a failed target
  never appears as a complete `dist/<ARCH>/` result.
- [x] Keep cook, staging, logs, manifests, and tool state under `.cache/`; keep
  final `dist/` output minimal and platform-native.
- [x] Make each published `dist/<ARCH>/` self-contained enough to copy elsewhere
  before deleting the repository workspace if the user wants.

## Dependencies

No explicit task dependency is required beyond the declared repository and
platform prerequisites.

## Evidence

`tools/build/adapter-inbound/run.py` now validates exact
`shar.build.check.v1` and
`shar.build.arch.v1` decisions, reruns `check.py --revalidate`, and rejects
duplicate
or drifted target records, and verifies each selected platform through Turnkey
without `UpdateIfNeeded` before invoking `BuildCookRun`. The canonical matrix
maps x86-64 to Unreal `x64`, ARM64 to `arm64`, and Linux ARM64 to the dedicated
`LinuxArm64` target; tvOS and macOS Intel are not admitted.

Direct runner invocation now delegates the saved architecture decision back to
`arch.py --revalidate` before consuming any target records. Explicit
architecture selection also rejects repeated target identifiers instead of
silently
deduplicating them before persistence. Focused regressions prove the exact saved
path is revalidated, a failed architecture revalidation stops the runner, and
`main()` performs that gate before target consumption. Architecture and
preflight evidence are consumed from stable single-link byte snapshots whose
SHA-256 is passed into child revalidation. `arch.py` and `check.py` refuse to
revalidate a different snapshot, and the runner requires the same bytes again
afterward, so an ABA path substitution cannot make the child validate one saved
decision while the runner consumes another. Direct revalidation also pins the
saved bytes across recomputation, so standalone success cannot race or trust an
external alias of the evidence it just validated.

Mobile publication now consumes the declared artifact kind instead of treating
any archive file as success. Android APKs must be valid ZIP containers with a
compiled resource binary-XML root `AndroidManifest.xml` whose root/string-pool/
start-element chunks are bounded and aligned. The first start element must
resolve through the bounded UTF-8/UTF-16 string pool to the exact `manifest`
root tag. That root must carry a no-namespace `package` raw-string attribute
whose value satisfies Android's lite-parser package-name grammar and length
bound, including its `android` special case. A non-empty no-namespace `split`
attribute is rejected so the published artifact remains one standalone base APK;
an absent or empty split identity remains base-package compatible. String/style
indices, string data ranges, and required terminators must remain inside the
string-pool chunk. The APK must also contain an ARM64 ELF native library at the
platform loader shape
`lib/arm64-v8a/lib<name>.so`; nested or non-library-shaped lookalikes do not
satisfy package admission. iOS IPAs must declare a nonempty
`CFBundleIdentifier` using Apple's documented ASCII alphanumeric/hyphen/period
character set, because iOS uses that identity during app-signature validation.
If `CFBundlePackageType` is present it must identify an app as `APPL`; omission
retains Apple's `.app`-extension fallback. The bundle must also declare a main
application executable in `Info.plist` whose Mach-O image contains ARM64.
Invalid containers,
wrong-architecture mobile binaries, zero-byte packages, and ambiguous multiple
APKs/IPAs cannot create a completed `dist/<ARCH>/` target. Mobile archive
inventories also reject rooted or Windows drive-qualified, parent/current
traversal, backslash, and empty-segment member paths plus
file/directory aliases and regular-file ancestors of nested members, so a valid
native entry cannot hide an unrealizable package tree beside it. Loader-critical
manifest, property-list, and native entries must also be regular files when ZIP
Unix metadata declares a file type; unrelated IPA symlinks remain admissible.
Every archive member is also decompressed through ZIP CRC validation before
package-specific
admission, so corruption outside the selected native entry still fails the
whole package. APK and IPA parsing also holds one stable single-link package
descriptor and requires the candidate path to retain that
identity through ZIP validation, so a transient external archive cannot
authorize different local bytes for publication. Universal Mach-O validation
consumes every
declared architecture record before accepting ARM64. FAT64 architecture records
must keep their reserved word zero, and each declared slice must have nonzero
in-bounds file data after the architecture table, honor its declared
power-of-two file alignment, and not overlap another slice. Selected
ARM64 slices must also begin at a 16-KiB page boundary and themselves begin with
an ARM64 thin Mach-O header whose CPU subtype matches the fat record after
masking subtype feature bits. Truncated, empty, overlapping, or mislabeled fat
slices cannot masquerade as complete macOS or iOS runtimes. Windows candidates
likewise require a non-empty
SHAR `.exe`; engine helper executables alone cannot satisfy publication. The
optional Windows shortcut adapter applies the same SHAR executable identity, so
original-game, helper, empty, prefixed-helper, or non-Shipping `.exe` files are
never auto-selected. Shortcut auto-discovery also requires `dist/` itself to
remain a real directory and traverses it with strict directory scans. Nested
redirects, hard-linked files, special entries, or scan failures therefore fail
instead of making a partial or externally aliased tree appear to contain one
unambiguous SHAR executable. Explicit shortcut targets also require one link.
Cached UAT manifests and Windows debug symbols are now required to be regular
files before they can leave the candidate tree; directory entries that merely
match `Manifest_*.txt` or `*.pdb` fail closed instead of becoming cached build
evidence. Every matching entry is type-checked before stale diagnostic caches
are pruned or any new diagnostic is moved, so malformed candidate evidence
preserves the previous successful cache. The required native/mobile artifact is
validated before those caches are replaced as well, so an otherwise well-formed
but non-runnable package cannot displace diagnostics from the last accepted
build.
Artifact entries also require one filesystem link at this boundary instead of
relying only on the earlier tree scan. Their bytes and filesystem identities are
captured through stable descriptors before the previous diagnostic caches are
mutated. Cache files are recreated exclusively from those captured bytes, and
the candidate source must still match its captured identity before removal, so a
check-to-move replacement cannot substitute external manifest or symbol content.
Linux candidates now require a non-empty SHAR native executable for both x64
and ARM64, and macOS candidates require a `.app` bundle with a non-empty SHAR
executable below `Contents/MacOS`. On POSIX hosts those native runtimes must
also retain execute permission. Desktop entrypoints must identify their native
binary format as ELF, Mach-O, or PE as appropriate, and the native header must
match the selected x64 or ARM64 target. ELF images must carry a complete ELF64
header with the current version, a System V/Linux OSABI, and zero ABI-version
and reserved identification bytes. Supported x64 and ARM64 images must use their
little-endian target encoding and omit reserved processor flags, and every image
must use an
executable/shared-object image type and a bounded ELF64 program-header table.
Every non-null program segment with file-backed bytes must stay inside the
image, and the nonconforming reserved `PT_SHLIB` segment type is rejected while
OS/processor-specific segment ranges remain admissible. `PT_DYNAMIC` file
images must contain complete ELF64 dynamic-array entries and at least one
mandatory `DT_NULL` terminator. `PT_INTERP`, when present, must occur once
before
every load segment and name one nonempty NUL-terminated interpreter path.
`PT_PHDR`, when present, must likewise occur
once before load segments, exactly describe the program-header table, and that
table must be mapped by a loadable segment. Optional `PT_TLS` templates must
remain read-only, keep initialized bytes no larger than the total template,
retain valid file/virtual alignment, and avoid wrapping the virtual address
space. At least one executable loadable segment must carry non-empty file-backed
bytes whose file range does not exceed its memory range or wrap the 64-bit
virtual address space.
Loadable ELF segments also retain the ABI alignment contract: `p_align` must be
zero, one, or a power of two, and aligned segments require congruent file
offsets and virtual addresses. `PT_LOAD` entries must also remain in ascending
virtual-address order. Linux desktop runtimes must also declare a
nonzero entrypoint inside
executable file-backed bytes of a load segment rather than its zero-fill tail or
the ELF/program-header metadata; Android loader-path libraries must be ELF
shared objects and may legitimately
declare no process entrypoint.
Truncated, relocatable, machine-only, or out-of-bounds
segment lookalikes fail.
Thin and universal
ARM64 Mach-O main images must carry a complete 64-bit header with a zero
reserved word, a bounded load-command table whose command sizes retain 64-bit
alignment and which rejects the 32-bit `LC_SEGMENT` form, an executable file
type, one bounded `__TEXT` segment beginning at
file offset zero, and
exactly one `LC_MAIN` or legacy `LC_UNIXTHREAD` entrypoint rather than only
carrying an ARM64 CPU field. Entrypoints inside the Mach-O header or
load-command table are rejected. `LC_MAIN` file offsets must otherwise remain
inside
executable file-backed bytes of the unique `__TEXT` segment instead of escaping
into a later executable mapping. Positive-size segment file and virtual-memory
ranges must remain pairwise disjoint. Segment load-command order must also
follow ascending VM addresses and nonzero file offsets, retaining Apple's
`__DWARF` exception. Admitted executables require exactly one `__LINKEDIT`
segment, and that `__LINKEDIT` mapping must have the greatest segment file
offset so the Apple loader sees it as the last segment. Apple link-edit data
commands must
use the exact 16-byte command structure, keep every positive-size declared
file payload inside the unique `__LINKEDIT` file mapping, keep those payload
ranges pairwise disjoint rather than merely file-bounded, and allow at most one
of each link-edit data command type. `LC_CODE_SIGNATURE` remains optional for
local/unsigned output under that same singleton rule. Object-file-only
`LC_LINKER_OPTION` and `LC_LINKER_OPTIMIZATION_HINT` commands are rejected from
these executable images. Classic `LC_DYLD_INFO`
and `LC_DYLD_INFO_ONLY` commands share that same containment/overlap gate across
all five of their file ranges, require the exact 48-byte structure, and may
appear only once in total. Present `LC_SYMTAB` metadata likewise requires its
exact 24-byte structure, a single command, and bounded 16-byte ARM64 symbol
entries plus a bounded string table through the shared `__LINKEDIT` range gate;
the symbol array must end at or before the string table begins.
Optional `LC_DYSYMTAB` metadata requires that `LC_SYMTAB` companion, uses one
exact 80-byte command, and bounds its TOC, 64-bit module, reference, indirect,
external-relocation, and local-relocation tables through the same range gate.
Its local, defined-external, and undefined symbol start/count groups must each
remain inside the companion `LC_SYMTAB` symbol count, including their end index.
A
structurally bounded `LC_LOAD_DYLINKER` must carry a nonempty, NUL-terminated
embedded path so the Apple loader does not classify it as a static executable;
it may appear only once, and executable images reject the dynamic-linker-only
`LC_ID_DYLINKER` identity command.
Present dylib-load and `LC_RPATH` commands must likewise keep their string
offsets beyond the fixed command structure and terminate the embedded string
inside the command; `LC_ID_DYLIB` is rejected in these executable images. A
present `LC_UUID` must use its exact 24-byte structure and appear at most once;
its presence is not required independently of Apple SDK format gates. Optional
`LC_ENCRYPTION_INFO_64` metadata must also use its 24-byte Mach-O64 structure,
appear once at most, and keep its encrypted file range bounded; the 32-bit form
is rejected in these 64-bit images. Optional `LC_NOTE` metadata must use its
exact 40-byte structure and keep its declared 64-bit file range bounded. Each
image must also carry one exact `LC_BUILD_VERSION`
platform declaration matching macOS or iOS admission, while legacy
`LC_VERSION_MIN_*` platform commands are rejected for these ARM64 targets.
`__TEXT` must be initially
readable/executable and `__LINKEDIT` read-only. Sections must remain inside
their
owning segment virtual
extent, and file-backed sections must also stay within the segment file extent.
Section records must retain the enclosing segment name, honor their declared
power-of-two virtual-address alignment, and keep any declared 8-byte relocation
entries inside the file. Zero-fill
section types must carry no file offset, while nonempty ordinary sections must
identify file-backed bytes. Segment VM
protections may use only
read/write/execute bits; nonzero initial mappings must be readable and remain a
