reject object-only linker, COMDAT, section-alignment, and extended-relocation
flags while retaining defined image memory flags. Nonempty section raw-data
sizes and file offsets must be multiples of file alignment while remaining
file-bounded.
Nonempty raw ranges must also begin at or after the declared header size and
follow section virtual-address order without overlap. A
nonzero program
entrypoint inside the intersection of executable loaded section memory and
file-backed section bytes rather than virtual zero-fill or raw-file padding or
only an `MZ`/machine lookalike. Runtime
size, execute permission, and
header parsing now share one stable single-link descriptor identity that must
still match the candidate path afterward. A transient external binary therefore
cannot authorize different local bytes that later reach publication. A renamed
executable or a binary for the wrong architecture cannot satisfy publication.
Plain data-only archives can no longer be published as successful desktop
packages on those platforms.

Publication also preflights an existing `dist/<ARCH>/` identity as a real
directory before any rename. A regression proves a colliding regular file is
left unchanged and cannot trigger a failed cleanup after a new package appears.
The hidden `.previous` publication identity is now checked for existence even
when its link target is missing, so a broken symlink or other malformed stale
backup cannot be ignored and left beside an otherwise successful target. A real
stale backup is not deleted, and a missing publication root is not created,
until final candidate admission succeeds. Rejection happens before the candidate
moves or publication state is mutated.

The `dist/` publication root is also required to be a real directory rather
than a symbolic link or Windows junction. A regression proves publication
rejects a linked output root before moving the candidate package.
The final swap binds that real publication-root identity, the selected target's
present-or-absent state, and the complete candidate tree identity across
last-mile candidate revalidation. Candidate root, directory, file, parent, or
target replacement is rejected before either the previous package or candidate
can move.

The candidate side of the transaction is likewise required to remain a real
cache-owned directory. A regression proves a linked candidate is rejected
before it can become a `dist/<ARCH>/` symlink to external content.

Project-state migration now validates both `.cache` and `.cache/build` as real
repository directories before creating or adopting Unreal-generated state.
Build preflight also requires every lexical repository ancestor of the canonical
`shar.uproject` descriptor to remain a real directory and parses the descriptor
from one stable single-link snapshot. That snapshot must remain unchanged
through preflight, and its SHA-256 is saved with the UAT project path, so
valid-association configuration drift is visible to direct check revalidation.
The build runner consumes that hash too: after check revalidation it requires
saved project path to equal the canonical repository descriptor, rechecks the
real parent/file boundary, and compares one descriptor-bound stable snapshot to
`project_sha256` before project-state migration or UAT. The descriptor identity
must continue to match the canonical path through the read, so redirected,
replaced, or post-revalidation project drift fails before it can become build
authority.
Regressions
cover linked cache and project ancestors so build state cannot escape the repo.

Canonical dependency, preflight, and architecture evidence writers now reject
linked `.cache`, `.cache/build`, or `.cache/build/data` ancestors before any
mutation. Rejection preserves existing evidence; architecture and preflight test
output overrides remain independent. Failed dependency-bootstrap recomputation
invalidates stale canonical evidence but preserves a pre-existing test-output
override that the failing invocation never published. Failed preflight
recomputation applies the same ownership rule: stale canonical check evidence is
removed, while an independent output override is preserved. Dependency bootstrap
additionally requires
real `.dependencies` and `.dependencies/build` tool storage even when its JSON
evidence path is overridden, because Cargo, rustup, and validator outputs remain
repository-owned side effects. Cached pinned rustup installers must be real
single-link files, and their checksum is read from one descriptor whose stable
filesystem identity must continue to match the lexical cache path through the
read. Downloads use an exclusively created PID staging file
before opening the network response. The checksum is computed from the exact
response bytes written to that owned handle, while pre-existing staging files or
links are preserved and fail closed. Reused or freshly installed repo-local Rust
toolchains also require real rustup/toolchain/bin directory ancestors and
single-link regular `cargo` and `rustc` leaves before bootstrap uses or records
them. Windows MSVC environment discovery likewise creates its PID-named command
file exclusively and preserves any pre-existing staging identity instead of
overwriting or deleting it. Cargo-produced validator build outputs may
retain Cargo's canonical `release/deps` hard link, but every filesystem link
must
be accounted for inside that repository-owned target tree. Published validator
identities remain real single-link files. Validator publication also creates its
PID staging identity exclusively, preserves a pre-existing staging file or link,
and copies from one descriptor-bound Cargo output whose filesystem identity must
remain stable through the copy. A stale or redirected staging path therefore
cannot overwrite external content or become the published validator. Preflight
repeats the real-directory
and single-link checks before trusting either validator. Each validator hash is
also streamed from one descriptor whose stable filesystem identity must continue
to match the canonical dependency path through the read. The binary hash is
rechecked after its validator source closure is fingerprinted, so replacement
during that longer scan cannot move binary authority outside repository-owned
storage. Preflight also defers selecting and validating the deep-source binary
until the fast manifest gate succeeds, reducing the interval between deep
validator verification and execution.
Canonical `arch.json`, `check.json`, and `dependencies.json` identities are also
required to be real regular files when already present. Build preflight repeats
the real `.cache/build/data` ancestor checks before consuming
`dependencies.json`,
so a post-bootstrap parent symlink or junction cannot redirect dependency
authority to external JSON. Preflight parses
and hashes one stable byte snapshot and requires the canonical dependency file
to retain one filesystem link and the same bytes through source and engine
validation, preventing either an external hard-link alias or a mid-check
replacement from binding saved evidence to JSON that did not select the
validators. The canonical game manifest is likewise required to remain below
real `game/` and `game/manifest/` directories, captured as one stable
single-link
snapshot before validation, required to retain the same bytes through preflight,
and hashed from that exact snapshot for saved evidence.
Direct saved-check revalidation applies the same single-link rule.
Linked or malformed saved evidence now fails before it can be consumed,
replaced,
or cleaned up.
Their PID-named atomic staging files are now created exclusively. A pre-existing
staging identity is preserved and causes the writer to fail closed instead of
being truncated or removed before the final atomic replace.
The shared `.cache/build/run` directory and each per-target work root are also
required to be real directories before Turnkey or UAT writes reports, logs,
staging data, or candidate packages.
Candidate and staging scratch roots are now preflighted before cleanup and
recreation too. Linked or malformed stale scratch identities fail before UAT,
rather than surviving ignored cleanup and reaching packaging.
Each UAT invocation now also validates its own child write roots: existing log
files must be real files with exactly one filesystem link. Existing logs are
opened without truncation, descriptor/path identity is compared, and only then
are bytes truncated through that owned descriptor; new logs use exclusive
creation. This prevents a check-to-open replacement from redirecting UAT output.
Meanwhile,
`automation-saved/`, its `logs/` child, and the local DDC must be real
directories. Linked identities fail before UAT starts, so cached tool output
cannot be redirected through a pre-existing work-tree identity. Turnkey SDK
evidence is likewise required to be a real report file with one filesystem link
both before stale-report cleanup and after UAT returns. Redirected or
hard-linked
`turnkey.txt` evidence cannot make external content satisfy the SDK-validity
gate. Report decoding also binds one descriptor identity to the cache path
before and after the UTF-8 read, so a post-UAT check-to-read replacement cannot
substitute external SDK evidence. The report must contain a platform-anchored
valid SDK row; a prefixed lookalike platform name cannot satisfy another
target's gate.
After UAT returns, the complete candidate tree is traversed without following
links and rejects symbolic links, junctions, hard-linked regular files, or
special filesystem entries before manifests/debug symbols are moved. Directory
open failures and per-entry metadata failures both stop that traversal through a
runner-owned error instead of exposing a partial inventory. Candidate artifact
and diagnostic-cache discovery reuse the file/directory result of that strict
traversal instead of recursively globbing the package again, so an
unreadable nested directory cannot disappear from runtime admission or debug
symbol classification through glob scan-error suppression. A failed strict scan
also leaves the previous diagnostic caches unchanged. The final publication
transaction repeats that tree gate and, for real target builds, repeats native
or mobile artifact admission after diagnostic caching. A runtime replaced by a
regular but wrong-format or wrong-architecture file after the first validation
therefore cannot reach the atomic swap. The real publication-root directory
identity is also captured after stale-backup cleanup and must remain identical
after final candidate validation, so replacing `dist/` with a symlink cannot
redirect the package swap outside the repository.

A real Windows AMD64 Shipping integration build completed successfully through
C++ compilation, cook, Pak/IoStore, stage, package, and archive in 29m10s. Its
unpublished test candidate contained 30 files totaling about 689 MiB, including
`shar.exe`, project content, required Engine files, manifests, and notices. The
integration driver intentionally stopped before `_publish`, so it did not create
`dist/` or persist a test architecture choice.

Fixture validation proved empty candidates cannot replace an existing published
target and valid candidates replace it transactionally with rollback support.
An injected failure while deleting the post-swap previous-package backup now
rolls the swap back as well: the prior `dist/<ARCH>/` tree is restored, the new
package returns to its cache candidate path, and no failed run leaves the new
publication exposed.
Turnkey validation also confirmed the installed Windows x64 SDK as valid. Build
preflight now reads Unreal `Build.version` through one stable single-link file
identity, so a transient external descriptor cannot supply the required 5.8.1
version tuple. The resolved engine root and every engine-internal directory
between it and `Build.version` must also be real rather than a symlink or
junction. The
host-native Unreal editor identity has the same internal-parent rule, must be a
regular file rather than merely an existing filesystem entry and, on POSIX
hosts, must have execute permission. The runner similarly requires a real engine
root and real internal parents for its host `RunUAT` launcher, then requires the
launcher itself to be a non-linked file and executable on POSIX before Turnkey
starts. Directory, redirected, or non-executable launchers cannot become build
authority. UAT logs are redirected beneath
`.cache/build/run/<target>/automation-saved/`. A
second real Shipping build completed successfully in 16m58s with the local DDC
and Zen data rooted under `.cache`; its stage and archive matched the first
successful package size. The packaged Shipping executable remained alive for an
8-second NullRHI/no-audio/unattended liveness probe, after which the exact test
PID was terminated cleanly.

Project-generated `Binaries/`, `DerivedDataCache/`, `Intermediate/`, and
`Saved/` resolve through runner-scoped directory links to
`.cache/build/project-state/`. Exact project-root ignore entries cover those
link identities while attached because Git's directory-only rules do not match
symlinks. Normal and error exits detach the links without deleting cached state,
so canonical Jig traversal sees no project-root boundary afterward. Migration
preflights all four identities, adopts
legacy real directories by same-filesystem rename, rejects competing canonical
state, and rolls back earlier moves if a later link fails. Standard-library
regressions cover adoption, reuse, dual-authority rejection, and injected
partial failure. On the real Windows workspace the first `Intermediate` rename
hit a transient `WinError 5`; rollback restored all four original directories,
and one controlled retry migrated all four successfully. A pinned Python 3.14.6
`run.py --validate-only` then revalidated build evidence and Turnkey reported
`windows-x64` SDK valid with all four junctions still targeting the cache.

The dependency bootstrap now also binds `validate-game` evidence to a SHA-256
fingerprint of its local Rust source closure, workspace manifest, and lockfile.
Source-closure traversal is explicit and strict in both bootstrap and preflight,
so an unreadable nested source directory cannot silently disappear from the
fingerprint under recursive-glob error suppression. Redirected, hard-linked, or
special source entries also fail instead of being omitted from the digest. Each
hashed source file is read through one descriptor whose device, inode,
timestamps, size, and link count must match the lexical path before and after
the read. This prevents scan-to-read substitution or external alias mutation
from entering validator evidence. Bootstrap fingerprints both validator source
closures before
Cargo starts and requires the same fingerprints after both builds and after
publication, so source drift cannot bind a previously built validator to newer
source evidence. This prevents an old byte-intact validator from surviving a
checkout that changes the canonical game-manifest contract; tests prove source
drift, build/publication drift, scan failure, redirect rejection, and legacy
evidence all fail closed.

A current Linux x64 Shipping integration attempt reached native UBT compilation
after canonical CPython 3.14.6 preflight and a valid Turnkey SDK check, then
reproduced Unreal Build Accelerator POSIX detour failures while writing the
cache-backed `Intermediate` tree: UBA reported cross-process rename-while-open
assertions and replacement-race copies through the project-state directory
link. UE 5.8.1 exposes the supported UBT `-NoUBA` switch through BuildCookRun's
`-UbtArgs` forwarding path. Linux build arguments now pass
`-UbtArgs=-NoUBA` so this repository-owned linked cache layout uses native
compiler filesystem behavior. Linux also passes `-SkipBuildEditor`: preflight
already requires a real executable host editor, so source-tree packaging can use
that editor for cooking instead of redundantly rebuilding it. A bounded UE 5.8.1
probe reduced the native agenda to `shar` plus `UnrealPak`; other target
families retain their existing build controls. The runner also takes a
repository-local advisory lock before revalidation or project-state migration,
so a concurrent invocation cannot reset target scratch or detach links beneath
an active build. The lock is held by the open descriptor rather than file
existence: POSIX `flock` and Windows runtime file locking release it
automatically when the process exits, avoiding stale lock-file authority after
crashes.

UAT now runs in its own host-native process group: a new POSIX session or a
Windows process group. Real Linux compilation exposed two stricter interruption
requirements. First, UBT creates its own process group and compiler descendants
can create new sessions, so killing only the RunUAT process group allowed an
orphaned UBT to keep compiling after project-state links detached. POSIX cleanup
now snapshots the full descendant tree by parentage before signaling, sends
`SIGTERM` to every captured PID, and escalates only surviving PIDs to `SIGKILL`
after a bounded wait. Windows retains `taskkill /T /F` descendant-tree
termination.

Second, a shell-launched background runner inherited both an ignored and
blocked `SIGINT`; merely installing a Python handler changed the disposition
but left the signal pending forever. Runner entry now explicitly unblocks
`SIGINT` and
`SIGTERM` on POSIX, installs controlled handlers for both signals, and restores
the caller mask and dispositions when `main` returns. A background probe showed
`SigBlk` change from signal 2 set to zero and then exited immediately on
`SIGINT`.

The same interruption contract now covers saved-evidence revalidation.
A live `SIGTERM` during `check.py --revalidate`, while it owned
`validate-source-deep`, reproduced a pre-UAT orphan: the runner and direct
