That sixth stop exposed one stricter Linux cleanup race. The runner and its
known descendant snapshot exited and all project-state links detached, but an
immediate process audit still captured one Shipping `clang++` process adopted
by PID 1. It disappeared before the next audit, but returning while it was still
active violated the runner-owned-process contract. An ancestry snapshot cannot
close that race because a descendant may fork after the snapshot and lose its
parent chain before the runner can observe it. Managed children now inherit a
unique `SHAR_BUILD_RUNNER_CHILD` token. Linux cleanup retains the normal
descendant-tree termination, then scans `/proc/*/environ` for that exact token
and force-stops any remaining tagged process until none remain. The token is
fresh per managed child and does not rely on executable names, paths, process
groups, or parentage. A real synthetic probe spawned a tagged new-session
child, let its parent exit so PID 1 adopted the child, then confirmed the token
sweep discovered and removed that orphan. Unit regressions cover token copying
and a tagged PID that escapes the ancestry snapshot.

